  "worked_solution": "We know that the denominator ${y}^{2}+2$ is positive for all values of $y.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${y}^{2}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le 4y \\\\ ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot 4y \\\\ 4{y}^{3}+11y-5 & \\le 4{y}^{3}+8y \\\\ 11y-5 & \\le 8y \\\\ 3y-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ y & \\le \\,\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
